## Two substantive decisions

62c: PASS. The current 9208 persisted definition in
`ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/7a/editions/modern-us-neutral/lessons/alg7-c4-s1-n29/lesson.json`
at `p0195#10` says the fraction set is the complement of the integers within
the rationals, Q minus Z. The current source page 0195, block 10, agrees.
Their SHAs remain respectively
`b38813c7362bd0a41263c421360a95bfa3c156f4c8c5112fc65750ae65b1cb47` and
`582d35cf8002afb72d9a21910bc2df6649e1b7bfb6062a0378666e4b35df567a`.
Thus -1/2 is in A but not in positive B, while the positive integer 1 is in B
but not in A. These independently refute both containments without needing an
irrational counterexample. The revised display gives exactly that conclusion
and reason, removing the unsupported conditional alternatives and definition
HOLD. Its notes are consistent. Parts a/b and ungraded remain unchanged.

63b: PASS. Current persisted `alg7-c1-s2-n6/lesson.json`, `p0033#3`, and source
page 0033 block 3 retain the exact truth-set example: "a two-digit number
smaller than 15" holds only for 10,11,12,13,14, not other values. Their SHAs are
`2573baa15fdd091fe3209d39947dc2fd2d19d4f8397ca48d3f59958a3f938519` and
`996540f8439b7e091b95899fcc2aae1fc68a4505ff75d90a34b6b08a842288ab`.
The book's positive 10..99 convention is therefore evidence, not a newly added
condition. A enumerates 10,20,30,40,50,60,70,80,90; B enumerates
15,30,45,60,75,90. Comparing these sets yields intersection {30,60,90} and
union {10,15,20,30,40,45,50,60,70,75,80,90}. The revised unconditional display
and notes match; the signed branch is gone. Part a, ungraded, and freedom of
member order are retained; no fixed-order automatic key is introduced.

The structured delta contains exactly four changed values: 62/63 displayAnswer
and notes. All other values, answer strings, and 27 automatic parts are unchanged.
